<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sirkap</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sirkap is the second city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, laid out on a grid plan in the 2nd century BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greeks</a> and rebuilt under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> and Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. Excavations by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a> uncovered streets, houses, stupas and the double-headed eagle shrine, showing a mix of Greek, Indian and Iranian styles. It is part of the Taxila World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sirkap.jpg" alt="Sirkap" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dawoodmajoka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Double-Headed_Eagle_Stupa_at_Sirkap_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Athena and Apollo, but also took part in Indian religions: Heliodorus, ambassador of Antialcidas, called himself a devotee of Vāsudeva on his pillar at Vidisha around 113 BCE, the first known inscription of the Bhagavata movement, and <b>Sirkap</b>, founded by Demetrius, mixed Greek and Indian elements without segregation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They patronised Buddhism, as the Bimaran reliquary and the Mathura Lion Capital show, and Taxila's city at <b>Sirkap</b> flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
